WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.706
Treating time as a resource starts with a simple fact: every hour is finite,

00:00:04.706 --> 00:00:06.680
nonrenewable, and already being spent somewhere.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.957
Time does not wait in storage until we feel ready to use it.

00:00:10.957 --> 00:00:14.247
It moves through work, learning, family life, operations, maintenance, recovery,

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:16.220
and the small transitions between them.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.168
Resource thinking asks where that time is going, what it is supporting,

00:00:20.168 --> 00:00:21.813
and what else it prevents.

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:25.761
Time is not the same thing as attention, energy, or calendar availability.

00:00:25.761 --> 00:00:29.709
A free hour after a difficult meeting may not have the same

00:00:29.709 --> 00:00:33.328
value as a protected hour at the start of the day.

00:00:33.328 --> 00:00:37.276
A person can technically have time and still lack the focus, context,

00:00:37.276 --> 00:00:39.579
or capacity to do demanding work well.

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.842
That distinction matters because many planning failures begin by treating every open block as equally useful.

00:00:44.842 --> 00:00:48.132
This is not a guilt-based way to talk about productivity.

00:00:48.132 --> 00:00:52.080
The goal is not to shame people for rest, family commitments, interruptions,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.067
or imperfect execution.

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:57.344
The goal is to make time allocation visible enough to manage it deliberately.

00:00:57.344 --> 00:00:59.647
When time use is invisible, defaults decide.

00:00:59.647 --> 00:01:02.937
Calendars fill, messages expand, urgent work crowds out important work,

00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:06.556
and people blame themselves for a system they never actually designed.

00:01:06.556 --> 00:01:08.859
A better frame is strategic resource management.

00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:10.175
Leaders, teams, students, parents,

00:01:10.175 --> 00:01:14.451
and individual contributors all make time tradeoffs whether they name them or not.

00:01:14.451 --> 00:01:18.070
The question is whether those tradeoffs support the outcomes that matter.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.702
Time can be planned, protected, reviewed, and reallocated.

00:01:20.702 --> 00:01:23.663
It cannot be recovered after it has been spent,

00:01:23.663 --> 00:01:27.940
which is why treating it as a resource changes the quality of decisions.
